WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:18.060
This is a ciliate, just a eukaryotic microbe
waving its cilia around under our microscope.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.750
And this is the same ciliate.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.940
And yup, here it is again…

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.140
…and again.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.960
But as you’re probably noticing, while the
rough outline of this organism seems the same

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:36.719
from shot to shot, the ciliate itself and
the world around it clearly look very different.

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:39.960
Colors change, details are more apparent.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.559
In one case the organism seems to be lit from
within.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.920
On this channel,

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:53.859
we’re constantly flipping between different ways of capturing images of organisms.

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:57.660
So which one of them is what they actually
look like?

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.620
Well...none of them.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.130
Anything you see through a microscope is an
image, which in our case, means that everything

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:13.290
we show you on this channel, every frame,
is not the microbial world itself.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:19.030
It’s an interpretation of the life on the
other side of our objective, translated through

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:26.720
the lens into details, and shapes, and colors—all
affected by the way we light up the life we

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.200
want to see.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.820
Light is amazing, it’s also very weird.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:40.710
It travels in waves, and as it interacts with
particles and materials, it scatters and shifts.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:47.130
Even if we can’t actually see those light
waves in motion, so much of what we observe

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:54.590
in the world around us is rooted in the physical
properties that define those waves—like

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.530
how we can observe certain frequencies of
light as colors.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:05.120
But waves have far more to them than just
their frequency, and microscopy has combined

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:12.950
the resourcefulness of many different sciences
to use light to give us different ways to

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.780
peer into the microbial world.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.640
So let’s start simple.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.460
Good old fashioned white light.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:28.540
Early microscopists used oil lamps and sunlight
to see through their microscopes, and while

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:33.630
the technology has changed, the simplicity
of this has endured into the modern technique

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.710
of brightfield microscopy.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:41.020
It all starts with a source of light, though
modern microscopes have their lamps built in,

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:45.230
set up underneath the stage that holds
our sample.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:52.470
Light travels from the source through a condenser, which works to focus the light onto the sample above it.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:58.380
This focused light travels through the sample
towards the objective lens, which takes in

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:04.860
an image and magnifies it into these bright
backgrounds with organisms, sometimes rendered

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.080
transparent by the intensity of the light.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.520
You might say that this is as close as we
get to seeing what the microcosmos actually

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.530
looks like, but that would be like taking
a 2000 watt light bulb into your living room

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.210
and saying, “this is what my home looks like.”

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:27.920
Light affects things, and we’re not even
shining light on these organisms, we’re

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.790
shining light through them.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:35.940
Now, Brightfield might seem relatively simple,
but that simplicity has been incredibly powerful

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:42.050
in allowing scientists old and new to wade
through microscopic waters.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:47.340
Still, there are limitations to consider with
any scientific technique, and one of the major

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:55.380
challenges for brightfield microscopy, particularly
when looking at microbes, is contrast.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.680
Pigmented organisms are easy to visualize
against the bright background, but in cases

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:06.240
where the organism has been rendered transparent,
it can be harder to distinguish their bodies

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.860
from the rest of the world they inhabit.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:14.460
Scientists can navigate these challenges using
stains that make certain structures more visible,

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:20.840
but for our purposes, we like to avoid stains
because they can affect the microbes themselves.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.590
There are other ways though to contend with
this challenge, one of which is built on one

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:32.410
of those simple-yet-strange properties of
our world: you don’t always need to shine

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.460
light directly onto an object to see it.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:42.740
This technique is called darkfield microscopy,
which sounds like it must be almost the opposite

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.350
of brightfield microscopy.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.120
It’s not.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:52.150
The two techniques are actually very similar:
light travels from a source through a condenser,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:57.090
goes through the sample, and then it travels
into the objective lens, producing the image

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.260
we see.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:04.431
But what we want in darkfield microscopy is
for the beam of light to hit the sample, but

00:05:04.431 --> 00:05:06.030
not our eye.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:13.580
So, in darkfield microscopy, a circular disk
is placed inside the condenser, blocking the

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:21.240
central part of the light from shining through
the sample and into our eye..or, our camera..

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:27.020
This means that when there is no sample on
the slide, all you see is black.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:33.830
But the disk doesn’t block all of the light:
there is still a hollow cone of light that

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:41.080
travels around the disk, unable to reach the
objective or our eyes, but that still hits

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.080
the sample.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:50.740
When it does, those microscopic, transparent
bodies scatter those hidden rays into our view.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:57.680
And as they do, an image of their bodies forms
against a dark background, providing us with

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.410
this almost cinematic footage.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:07.590
Another method to get better contrast than
brightfield microscopy is called phase contrast

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:12.960
microscopy, and it’s built on working with
a property of light that we can’t actually

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.729
directly experience.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:21.470
Microbes , or really anything, that is easily
visually observed with brightfield microscopy

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:27.320
are called amplitude objects because as light
passes through them, the amplitude of the

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:31.770
light wave changes, which we see as changes
in light intensity.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:36.800
But there is another class of specimens: these
are called phase objects.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:43.800
As light passes through these objects, the
waves slow down and shift slightly in phase

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.600
compared to the unaffected light around it.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.760
And if you’re wondering what that means
in terms of what we can see, that’s the

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:56.350
issue: our eyes don’t process these differences
in phase.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:01.820
And so in the final image, these objects (or
in our case, organisms) are very difficult to see.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:12.940
Well, in the 1930s, a physicist named Frits
Zernike developed a method to shift the direct

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:20.259
light just slightly enough so that these changes
in phase could actually be translated into

00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:27.389
changes in amplitude, producing an image of
these formerly hard-to-see phase objects by

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:31.000
essentially treating them as amplitude objects.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.380
There is a lot of physics in this that we
are not going to get into, but the result

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:40.770
was so important that it would eventually
win Zernike the Nobel Prize in Physics.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:47.300
And of course, selfishly, we here appreciate
his work because it lets us see more of our

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.080
more hidden microbial friends.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:56.600
And for the last type of microscopy we’ll
go over today, we’re going to be getting

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.540
into another property of light that we can’t
directly see,

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:05.580
and this one can make the microcosmos glow.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.979
Most of the light we see has an electrical
field that vibrates in all sorts of planes

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:13.449
relative to the direction the light is traveling in.

00:08:13.449 --> 00:08:19.860
But that vibration can be restricted to just
one plane, and when that happens, the light

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.520
is said to be polarized.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:26.380
We can’t see the difference between polarized
and unpolarized light.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:31.050
Now you might see the difference in how the
world looks when you’re wearing polarized

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:36.949
sunglasses, but these changes are brought
about by changes in color or intensity, not

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:40.310
the polarization of the light itself.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:43.680
So when does polarized light help microscopy?

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:51.110
Well, a lot of materials stay the same optically-speaking,
no matter what direction you shoot light at them from.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:56.810
But there are certain materials where specific
properties, like how fast light travels through

00:08:56.810 --> 00:09:02.010
them, can vary depending on which way the
light is striking them.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:09.330
These materials called optically anisotropic,
can also take in a ray of light and divide

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:13.120
it into two separate beams.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:19.110
By aiming polarized light at our sample and
then reconstructing an image based on how

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:25.360
the various parts of the organism interacts
with that restricted light, particularly by

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:31.100
how it might cause that light to split, we
can see more of these optically anisotropic

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.220
materials in action.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:38.620
In our case, it often takes the form of shiny
internal crystals.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:49.880
So we’ve given you the big overview of what
these different techniques can do, let’s

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:55.060
go back and look at what that means for that
original ciliate we started with.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.850
Here it is under brightfield microscopy.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:03.060
The background is bright, the image produced
by changes in light amplitude that allow us

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.940
to see the overall shape.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.830
But some of the detail is hard to make out.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:14.250
Under darkfield though, the contrast increases
and some of these details become more obvious,

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:22.330
displaying compartments and cilia in greater
detail that are also apparent under phase contrast.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:28.200
And then under polarized light, the crystals
that blended in with the organism previously

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.930
are now visible and vibrant.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.820
Now, of course, there are many other microscopy
techniques that use light in many different

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:45.140
ways, but we think it’s incredible that
with just these four, the world of the microcosmos

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:52.960
looks almost like different universes, wrapped
up into one invisible world around us.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:00.850
The journey, it seems, is not just about what
you see, but also how you see it.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:07.930
And ultimately, none of these views are what
the microcosmos actually looks like, either

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.649
that, or all of them are.

00:11:10.649 --> 00:11:17.490
Our brains play tricks on us to make us believe
that the world looks one way, but the world

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:23.100
looks different at night than in the day,
and both of those things have more to do with

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:29.230
the physiology of our eyes and our brains
than with objective reality.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:35.480
Asking what a microbe actually looks like
is, to some extent, forcing our own experience

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:38.920
onto something that is beyond it.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.960
Which is not something I ever would have thought
of if it weren’t for this little youtube channel.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.860
This is the last episode of our first season
of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:56.480
It’s been really wonderful, and don’t
worry, we’re just going to take a week off and

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:01.860
then we will be back with our second season,
featuring more of our microbial buddies and

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.840
their various antics.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:11.780
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:16.940
And a special thank you to all of these people,  our patrons on Patreon.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:22.420
Who make it possible for us to take such a deep and interested look at this wonderful world.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:24.580
Thank you everybody for being a part of that.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:27.760
If you want to see more from our Master of Microbes, James,

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.640
you can check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:36.800
And if you want to be here and ready for next season, go to YouTube.com/microcosmos

